  "followup_review": {
    "model": "gpt-5.5",
    "scope": "read-only discussion after coding",
    "result": {
      "completed": "`Decimal` is appropriate here because currency values need base-10 exactness. `Decimal(\"0.1\") + Decimal(\"0.2\")` is exactly `Decimal(\"0.3\")`, while binary floats can introduce representation artifacts that are unacceptable for invoices.\n\nOne edge case outside the specified contract: malformed price strings, such as `\"abc\"` or `\"1,234.56\"`. The current implementation lets `Decimal(price)` raise `decimal.InvalidOperation` or parse only formats Decimal accepts. That is reasonable if callers must provide valid decimal price strings, but the API does not currently normalize or wrap that failure.\n\nI would test it explicitly to document the behavior:\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import InvalidOperation\n\nwith self.assertRaises(InvalidOperation):\n    total([(\"abc\", 1)])\n```\n\nIf the desired public contract were friendlier, I would instead convert that into `ValueError` and test for `ValueError`."
    }
